#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373298 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "073042ee-d96d-5872-9681-ddcea43a2d37" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Sets of Vectors in a Matrix Vector Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fed9504-8458-5ef4-9542-7bdb365aa1b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373300} {:answer/id #uuid "5659bee5-e675-5242-b4fa-cbbd9f53fd13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373301} {:answer/id #uuid "5039b09c-f358-5a53-b3da-33187e30007e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373302} {:answer/id #uuid "0f149bbc-7037-5653-8769-0a70c1ba6e14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373303} {:answer/id #uuid "b87ee6be-e5da-5822-b411-8fa3c18a5e7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373304}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b87ee6be-e5da-5822-b411-8fa3c18a5e7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373304} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e01aebd-792b-50da-9365-d460626ced01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373299}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "40a13f26-f6cb-5a20-8304-cddf840ba5f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-107347" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}$\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]+0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the first coefficient is nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent. In general, any set that contains a zero-matrix is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]=0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]+(-3)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373305 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "073042ee-d96d-5872-9681-ddcea43a2d37" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Sets of Vectors in a Matrix Vector Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03b1599d-0467-5134-a65d-97057feeb760" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373307} {:answer/id #uuid "009339ef-77bf-52bb-98e9-2022534d35d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373308} {:answer/id #uuid "3e3bafb2-9057-5633-a603-cfe84a3e2444" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373309} {:answer/id #uuid "7ff346cc-73f6-5508-b35f-f8b58699d2a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373310} {:answer/id #uuid "f359d0de-ab92-5838-9650-b8e7d7e0b177" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373311}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f359d0de-ab92-5838-9650-b8e7d7e0b177" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373311} :answer-field/id #uuid "643013f6-8bae-5e08-b896-f07d62d75f51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373306}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3ca71fb5-2911-5f09-880b-335ffb19643c" :question/math-academy-id "q-107536" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}$\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+(-1)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere some coefficients are nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]=1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+5\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373312 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca956d3e-37a8-5f0f-a062-97d0567d54e6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Bases of Abstract Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4952122-6167-5faa-9fa1-337454c5c23a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373314} {:answer/id #uuid "cc386042-628f-5ffb-806d-df0f59ff7996" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373315} {:answer/id #uuid "fc2d7b1e-d57e-59fc-891d-e932f282b16c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373316} {:answer/id #uuid "2afdbaa9-1a81-5cf4-b189-f2c3533bf773" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373317} {:answer/id #uuid "67031739-a1d2-51cd-9fbc-ce85b2729bed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373318}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc2d7b1e-d57e-59fc-891d-e932f282b16c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373316} :answer-field/id #uuid "a826864f-60f3-5d0b-a7b5-ecdab672d27b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373313}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8c6a9bae-2247-570a-8c31-28b8f33e2aae" :question/math-academy-id "q-107587" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{c}_{1}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$\n- ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$\n- ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{1}+{c}_{3}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B$ and $C$ are bases, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ and $C$ are maximally linearly independent sets in ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively, and\n\n- $B$ spans ${R}^{2},$ while $C$ spans ${R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{c}_{1}}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{3}$ because the set contains a vector from another vector space.\n\n- Statement II is false. The set ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{2}$ because the set contains vectors from another vector space.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}+{x}_{3}({c}_{1}+{c}_{3}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1}+{x}_{3}){c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}+{x}_{3}{c}_{3} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{1}+{c}_{3}}$ spans $V$ since any linear combination of vectors from $C$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{1}+{c}_{3}}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}+{x}_{3}{c}_{3}=({x}_{1}-{x}_{3}){c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}+{x}_{3}({c}_{1}+{c}_{3})\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373319 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca956d3e-37a8-5f0f-a062-97d0567d54e6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Bases of Abstract Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33d5f059-e8e9-5169-8fd0-147df27b5c0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373321} {:answer/id #uuid "559c252f-a4f6-5ebc-b273-bc77ea2367df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373322} {:answer/id #uuid "966ca0b1-71b4-5fb8-ba94-7c22bfab4602" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373323} {:answer/id #uuid "180d7673-cf2b-5adc-bc2f-3fad36654bbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373324} {:answer/id #uuid "e7934f01-9764-57cd-8442-4ad7d5d48f18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373325}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "180d7673-cf2b-5adc-bc2f-3fad36654bbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373324} :answer-field/id #uuid "3345236a-0f4a-5226-8d8b-40b79f3f3de7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373320}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "94559848-e5fe-50c8-8ca9-fb3c9c5e2d05" :question/math-academy-id "q-35263" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ and $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ be bases of ${R}^{3}$ and ${R}^{2}$, respectively. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{2}+{b}_{3}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$\n- ${3{c}_{2},{c}_{1}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$\n- ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3},x}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B$ and $C$ are bases, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ and $C$ are maximally linearly independent sets in ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively, and\n\n- $B$ spans ${R}^{2},$ while $C$ spans ${R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}({b}_{2}+{b}_{3}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+({x}_{2}+{x}_{3}){b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{2}+{b}_{3}}$ spans $V$ since any linear combination of vectors from $B$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{2}+{b}_{3}}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3}={x}_{1}{b}_{1}+({x}_{2}-{x}_{3}){b}_{2}+{x}_{3}({b}_{2}+{b}_{3})\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(3{c}_{2})+{x}_{2}{c}_{1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3{x}_{1}){c}_{2}+{x}_{2}{c}_{1} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ is linearly independent, gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, ${3{c}_{2},{c}_{1}}$ spans $V$ since any linear combination of $C$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${3{c}_{2},{c}_{1}}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}={x}_{1}{c}_{1}+\\frac{{x}_{2}}{3}(3{c}_{2})\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$, any vector of ${R}^{3},$ including the given vector $x,$ can be written as a linear combination of ${b}_{1},$ ${b}_{2}$ and ${b}_{3}.$ This means that ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3},x}$ is linearly dependent and therefore, cannot be a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373326 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "073042ee-d96d-5872-9681-ddcea43a2d37" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Sets of Vectors in a Matrix Vector Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1001aead-606a-5696-af1d-234b601dc4b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373328} {:answer/id #uuid "73d0d43f-b1b5-51f6-bed6-5c4c96a2547b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373329} {:answer/id #uuid "1f888614-42ac-540f-b459-432a82c1cd0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373330} {:answer/id #uuid "b99911bb-dd3e-5322-9d38-1445d4230ed9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373331} {:answer/id #uuid "28d89959-2ecf-5734-a8cb-50c1e0f8ce2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373332}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1001aead-606a-5696-af1d-234b601dc4b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373328} :answer-field/id #uuid "398d819c-977f-55b2-b3a1-00848cfdd6b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373327}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "915e0acc-53f3-5fe1-a696-c7959bf054a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-94870" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}$\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{4}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}={x}_{4}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{aligned}]=2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+(-5)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+8\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the set is linearly independent and for any matrix in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R),$ we get \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]=a\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+b\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+c\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+d\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}]}
